    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-centre-for-international-agricultural-research-act-1982-sec-29c",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "29C Termination of appointment",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-centre-for-international-agricultural-research-act-1982#sec-29c"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "australian-centre-for-international-agricultural-research-act-1982.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "australian-centre-for-international-agricultural-research-act-1982.pdf, p.27",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Governor-General may terminate the appointment of the CEO:\n(a) for misbehaviour; or\n(b) if the CEO is unable to perform the duties of the CEO’s\noffice because of physical or mental incapacity; or\n(c) if the CEO’s conduct or behaviour amounts to serious\nmisconduct by the CEO.\n(1A) Without limiting the circumstances in which the CEO’s conduct or\nbehaviour amounts to serious misconduct by the CEO, the CEO’s\nconduct or behaviour amounts to serious misconduct by the CEO\nin the following circumstances:\n(a) both:\n(i) the conduct or behaviour constitutes a breach of the\nAPS Code of Conduct; and\n(ii) the Minister is satisfied that the breach is serious\nenough to justify termination of the CEO’s\nappointment;\n(b) the conduct or behaviour constitutes unlawful discrimination\n(within the meaning of the Australian Human Rights\nCommission Act 1986);\n(c) an investigation report (within the meaning of the National\nAnti-Corruption Commission Act 2022) includes a finding or\nopinion that the CEO has engaged, is engaging or will\nengage in:\n(i) corrupt conduct (within the meaning of that Act); or\n(ii) conduct that could constitute or involve corrupt conduct.\n(2) The Governor-General must terminate the appointment of the CEO\nif:\n(a) the CEO:\n(i) becomes bankrupt; or\n(ii) applies to take the benefit of any law for the relief of\nbankrupt or insolvent debtors; or\n(iii) compounds with his or her creditors; or",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The Governor-General may terminate the appointment of the CEO:\n(a) for misbehaviour; or\n(b) if the CEO is unable to perform the duties of the CEO’s\noffice because of physical or mental incapacity; or\n(c) if the CEO’s conduct or behaviour amounts to serious\nmisconduct by the CEO.\n(1A) Without limiting the circumstances in which the CEO’s conduct or\nbehaviour amounts to serious misconduct by the CEO, the CEO’s\nconduct or behaviour amounts to serious misconduct by the CEO\nin the following circumstances:\n(a) both:\n(i) the conduct or behaviour constitutes a breach of the\nAPS Code of Conduct; and\n(ii) the Minister is satisfied that the breach is serious\nenough to justify termination of the CEO’s\nappointment;\n(b) the conduct or behaviour constitutes unlawful discrimination\n(within the meaning of the Australian Human Rights\nCommission Act 1986);\n(c) an investigation report (within the meaning of the National\nAnti-Corruption Commission Act 2022) includes a finding or\nopinion that the CEO has engaged, is engaging or will\nengage in:\n(i) corrupt conduct (within the meaning of that Act); or\n(ii) conduct that could constitute or involve corrupt conduct.\n(2) The Governor-General must terminate the appointment of the CEO\nif:\n(a) the CEO:\n(i) becomes bankrupt; or\n(ii) applies to take the benefit of any law for the relief of\nbankrupt or insolvent debtors; or\n(iii) compounds with his or her creditors; or",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-centre-for-international-agricultural-research-act-1982-sec-29c-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-centre-for-international-agricultural-research-act-1982-sec-29c-ss-1a"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-centre-for-international-agricultural-research-act-1982-sec-29c-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "a7a0564bc3d83ce9204b5dda99a15bdeab1dd50a952fa113ae553a374fc75d53"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-centre-for-international-agricultural-research-act-1982#sec-29c",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "29C Termination of appointment",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 27,
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Governor-General may terminate the appointment of the CEO:\n(a) for misbehaviour; or\n(b) if the CEO is unable to perform the duties of the CEO’s\noffice because of physical or mental incapacity; or\n(c) if the CEO’s conduct or behaviour amounts to serious\nmisconduct by the CEO.\n(1A) Without limiting the circumstances in which the CEO’s conduct or\nbehaviour amounts to serious misconduct by the CEO, the CEO’s\nconduct or behaviour amounts to serious misconduct by the CEO\nin the following circumstances:\n(a) both:\n(i) the conduct or behaviour constitutes a breach of the\nAPS Code of Conduct; and\n(ii) the Minister is satisfied that the breach is serious\nenough to justify termination of the CEO’s\nappointment;\n(b) the conduct or behaviour constitutes unlawful discrimination\n(within the meaning of the Australian Human Rights\nCommission Act 1986);\n(c) an investigation report (within the meaning of the National\nAnti-Corruption Commission Act 2022) includes a finding or\nopinion that the CEO has engaged, is engaging or will\nengage in:\n(i) corrupt conduct (within the meaning of that Act); or\n(ii) conduct that could constitute or involve corrupt conduct.\n(2) The Governor-General must terminate the appointment of the CEO\nif:\n(a) the CEO:\n(i) becomes bankrupt; or\n(ii) applies to take the benefit of any law for the relief of\nbankrupt or insolvent debtors; or\n(iii) compounds with his or her creditors; or"
    },
